\section*{Abstract}
Cryptographic systems traditionally rely on algorithmic randomness, which remains vulnerable to predictive modeling and computational advances. Here we present a multi-physics simulator that couples vascular tissue dynamics, biorheological fluid flow, and astrophysical density fields within a unified Hamiltonian framework. Biological state vectors $(q_{bio}, p_{bio})$, astrophysical flux fields derived from FITS cubes, and interaction terms are combined into a composite Hamiltonian whose chaotic regimes serve as a non-deterministic entropy source for cryptographic key generation. Chaotic divergence is quantified via Lyapunov exponents and mapped into time-dependent key material through the Blackwell Encryption Layer (BEL). This architecture introduces a physics-anchored cryptographic paradigm with provenance, reproducibility, and Hamiltonian integrity. We outline the mathematical formulation, system architecture, and validation pathway required for laboratory assessment.

\section*{Main}
Modern cryptographic systems depend on pseudo-random number generators and algorithmic entropy sources. These approaches are increasingly susceptible to advances in computational modeling, simulation-based prediction, and hardware acceleration. A physics-anchored alternative, in which entropy is derived from chaotic divergence in multi-domain Hamiltonian systems, offers a fundamentally different security foundation.

The H-WAVE-CIE simulator integrates three physical regimes: vascular tissue networks, biorheological fluid flow, and astrophysical density fields. These domains are unified through a composite Hamiltonian,
\[
H = H_{bio}(q_{bio}, p_{bio}) + H_{astro}(q_{astro}, p_{astro}) + H_{interaction}(q_{bio}, q_{astro}),
\]
which governs the evolution of the full state vector
\[
x = (q_{bio}, p_{bio}, q_{astro}, p_{astro}).
\]

Chaotic regimes emerge when the system is driven through parameter sweeps and boundary conditions that induce sensitive dependence on initial conditions. Divergence is quantified using Lyapunov exponents,
\[
\lambda(t) = \frac{1}{t}\ln\frac{\|\delta x(t)\|}{\|\delta x(0)\|},
\]
and chaotic attractor trajectories are sampled to generate cryptographic keys $K(t)$.

The Blackwell Encryption Layer (BEL) maps chaotic trajectories and divergence profiles into key material, producing physics-anchored encrypted simulation streams. A provenance spine records Hamiltonian parameters, initial conditions, divergence metrics, and key evolution history, enabling reproducibility and laboratory verification.

\section*{Results}
Coupling biological and astrophysical domains produces chaotic regimes with high divergence density. Key material derived from these regimes exhibits non-deterministic behavior, high entropy, and sensitivity to initial conditions. The BEL module successfully converts chaotic attractor coordinates into cryptographic keys and applies XOR-based encryption to simulation payloads.

\section*{Discussion}
This work introduces a new class of cryptographic systems grounded in multi-physics Hamiltonian dynamics. Unlike algorithmic randomness, physics-anchored entropy cannot be reproduced without reconstructing the underlying physical regime. This provides a pathway toward cryptographic systems with inherent provenance, reproducibility, and resistance to brute-force simulation.

\section*{Methods}
\subsection*{Hamiltonian Formulation}
The composite Hamiltonian is defined as above. Gradients are computed as:
\[
\nabla H = \left(
\frac{\partial H}{\partial q_{bio}},
\frac{\partial H}{\partial p_{bio}},
\frac{\partial H}{\partial q_{astro}},
\frac{\partial H}{\partial p_{astro}}
\right).
\]

\subsection*{Numerical Integration}
A symplectic leapfrog integrator preserves Hamiltonian structure. Voxel-level solvers handle multi-scale interactions. FITS cube readers process astrophysical flux fields.

\subsection*{Chaos Quantification}
Lyapunov exponents are computed using standard divergence metrics. Chaotic attractor trajectories are sampled at discrete time intervals $\Delta t$.

\subsection*{Key Generation}
Key material is derived using:
\[
K(t_i) = g(x(t_i), \lambda(t_i)).
\]

